on now where ukraine's  logistics are very very difficult and  crucial basically to them being able to  hold on to where they are right now in  donbass  dw's nick conley there reporting from  central ukraine  well frank ledwich is a military analyst  and former intelligence officer with the  british army he joins us now from oxford  england frank ukraine says it  desperately needs more military hardware  and not just heavy weapons but basic  supplies like ammunition just how  under-equipped are the ukrainians at  this stage  they use thousands of shells every day  in what has been since the start and  remains essentially an artillery battle  and the problem they face in artillery  battles is every shell that creates a  field or wrecks a building doesn't  actually have any tactical effect is a  shell thrown away so what the west is  trying to do is give them precision  weaponry  as you said they're running out of of  the old the old weaponry ammunition  thing is of course as we've said for  weeks now they're not getting enough the  amounts they're getting for example of  rocket artillery strikes deep into the  russian logistics rear absolutely  essential to stop the supply of russian  shells coming forward and strike russian  artillery headquarters the  gifts and supplies that west have been  supplying are insufficient they're a  drop in the ocean of what's required to  the point actually even beyond that of  somewhat derisory particularly in rocket  artillery  now quite a bit of of weaponry has been  promised by the west why is that  weaponry not reaching ukraine faster  frank  of course some of it isn't isn't it  hasn't even left the uh left the gifting  countries yet  that those which have some of it is  locked in bottlenecks so there are  supply bottlenecks in western ukraine i  understand  uh some of it has been struck by russian  rockets uh rocket attacks although i  don't think that's been decisive and  some of it's been eroded away treated as  it were on the 